    <fr:route>/sss-0022/</fr:route>
    <fr:title text="What Connectives Perserve Finiteness?">What Connectives Perserve Finiteness?</fr:title>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>
  In order to bridge <fr:link href="/sss-001W/" title="Finite Unambiguity is Not Equivalent to Unambiguity" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-001W/" display-uri="sss-001W" type="local">the gap between finite unambiguity and unambiguity</fr:link>, we can try to restrict to grammars that have finitely many parse trees. We'd expect this to address the concern semantically, as that fixes the problem when the parses are interpreted in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbf {Set}]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors>
          <fr:author>
            <fr:link href="/stevenschaefer/" title="Steven Schaefer" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/stevenschaefer/" display-uri="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
          </fr:author>
        </fr:authors>
        <fr:date>
          <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
          <fr:month>2</fr:month>
          <fr:day>12</fr:day>
        </fr:date>
        <fr:source-path>/Users/stevenschaefer/forest/trees/public/sss-001X.tree</fr:source-path>
        <fr:uri>https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-001X/</fr:uri>
        <fr:display-uri>sss-001X</fr:display-uri>
        <fr:route>/sss-001X/</fr:route>
        <fr:title text="Sets That Are Isomorphic to Their Own Product">Sets That Are Isomorphic to Their Own Product</fr:title>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <html:p>
  Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex> be a set such that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X \cong  X \times  X]]></fr:tex>. Then either <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex> is the empty set, the singleton set, or an infinite set.
</html:p>
        <html:p>
We may read this intuitively as stating the fixed points of the squaring operation on cardinals has solutions <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[0]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[1]]></fr:tex>, and all infinite cardinals <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\kappa ]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
    <html:p>
  We can define <fr:link href="/sss-0021/" title="Internal Finiteness" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-0021/" display-uri="sss-0021" type="local">when a grammar is finite</fr:link>, and then try to prove that finiteness is preserved on some sane operations on grammars. For instance, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\And ]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\oplus ]]></fr:tex> , and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\to ]]></fr:tex> each preserve finiteness. I haven't proven this myself (which would be a useful exercise), but the following is substantiated in any topos with a natural numbers object <fr:link href="/johnstone-2002/" title="Sketches of an Elephant: A Topos Theory Compendium" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/johnstone-2002/" display-uri="johnstone-2002" type="local">[johnstone-2002]</fr:link>.
</html:p>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors>
          <fr:author>
            <fr:link href="/stevenschaefer/" title="Steven Schaefer" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/stevenschaefer/" display-uri="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
          </fr:author>
        </fr:authors>
        <fr:date>
          <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
          <fr:month>2</fr:month>
          <fr:day>12</fr:day>
        </fr:date>
        <fr:source-path>/Users/stevenschaefer/forest/trees/public/sss-0023.tree</fr:source-path>
        <fr:uri>https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-0023/</fr:uri>
        <fr:display-uri>sss-0023</fr:display-uri>
        <fr:route>/sss-0023/</fr:route>
        <fr:title text="Finite Cardinal Arithmetic in a Topos">Finite Cardinal Arithmetic in a Topos</fr:title>
        <fr:meta name="external">\citek{johnstone-2002}</fr:meta>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <html:p>
  In a topos with a natural numbers object <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(N, z , s)]]></fr:tex>, you can define finite cardinals as objects that arise as the pullback along a morphism <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[p : \top  \to  N]]></fr:tex> of the generic finite cardinal. Write <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[p]]]></fr:tex> for the cardinal corresponding to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[p]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
        <html:p>
  The above characterization is a little obtuse and does warrant some more explanation. One way to make it more concrete is that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[z] = \bot ]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[s \circ  n] = \top  \oplus  [n]]]></fr:tex>, and that finite cardinals warrant a nice induction principle. If <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> is a property expressible in the internal language such that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\bot ]]></fr:tex> satisfies <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex>, and that whenever <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> satisfies <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> then <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\top  \oplus  A]]></fr:tex> satisfies <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex>; then every finite cardinal satisfies <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex>. That is, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> forms a <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(z,s)]]></fr:tex>-closed subobject of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N]]></fr:tex> and thus <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P]]></fr:tex> is all of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
        <html:p>
  My working mental model is in a presheaf topos, where the natural numbers object can be defined explicity as <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Delta (\mathbb {N})]]></fr:tex> .
</html:p>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors>
              <fr:author>
                <fr:link href="/stevenschaefer/" title="Steven Schaefer" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/stevenschaefer/" display-uri="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
              </fr:author>
            </fr:authors>
            <fr:date>
              <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
              <fr:month>2</fr:month>
              <fr:day>12</fr:day>
            </fr:date>
            <fr:source-path>/Users/stevenschaefer/forest/trees/public/sss-0024.tree</fr:source-path>
            <fr:uri>https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-0024/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>sss-0024</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/sss-0024/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Constant Presheaf">Constant Presheaf</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
  The <html:em>constant</html:em> presheaf of a set <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex> on a category <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C]]></fr:tex> is a functor <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Delta (X) : C^{op} \to  \mathbf {Set}]]></fr:tex> such that
</html:p>
            <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\Delta (X)c \colonequals  X]]></fr:tex>
            <html:p>I often call this <html:em>the discrete presheaf for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex></html:em>, but I don't know if that's standard.</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <html:p>
 The finite cardinals with respect to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\Delta (\mathbb {N})]]></fr:tex> can be characterized then as,
</html:p>
        <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ [0] \colonequals  \bot  ]]></fr:tex>
        <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ [\mathsf {suc}(n)] \colonequals  \top  \oplus  [n] ]]></fr:tex>
        <html:p>These obey nice algebraic properties.</html:p>
        <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ [nm] \cong  [n] \times  [m] ]]></fr:tex>
        <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ [n + m] \cong  [n] \oplus  [m] ]]></fr:tex>
        <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ [n^{m}] \cong  [m] \to  [n] ]]></fr:tex>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
    <html:p>
  The case of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\otimes ]]></fr:tex> is more problematic. Semantically, for all <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[w : \mathrm {String}]]></fr:tex> we may bound the size of the set of parse trees
  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ {\mathopen {}\left \lvert (A \otimes  B) w\right \rvert \mathclose {}} \leq  \Sigma _{w_{1} \texttt { ++ } w_{2} = w} {\mathopen {}\left \lvert A w_{1}\right \rvert \mathclose {}} {\mathopen {}\left \lvert B w_{2}\right \rvert \mathclose {}}  ]]></fr:tex></html:p>
    <html:p>
Precisely knowing this bound isn't too important, but certainly it exists. We could capture this behavior by just adding an axiom that if <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex> are each finite, then <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A \otimes  B]]></fr:tex> is finite. Although, I'd rather not add an axiom.
</html:p>
    <html:p>
We could directly try to prove that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\otimes ]]></fr:tex> preserves finiteness. The statment would follow from showing that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\otimes ]]></fr:tex> preserves monomorphisms. That is, if <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f : A \hookrightarrow  C]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[g : B \hookrightarrow  D]]></fr:tex>, it suffices to show that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f \otimes  g : A \otimes  B \to  C \otimes  D]]></fr:tex> is a monomorphism. This would imply finiteness, because you may then apply this for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[C]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[D]]></fr:tex> as <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {Fin}]]></fr:tex>. However, you would also need to show that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {Fin} \otimes  \mathsf {Fin}]]></fr:tex> is finite, which isn't immediately clear.
</html:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
