            <fr:title text="Internal Finiteness">Internal Finiteness</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
Define a grammar <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> to be <html:em>finite</html:em> (or perhaps <html:em>subfinite</html:em>) if it is a subobject of grammar of finite cardinals.

<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[A \hookrightarrow  \mathsf {Fin}]]></fr:tex>

Where <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {Fin}]]></fr:tex> is defined as follows.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/stevenschaefer/" title="Steven Schaefer" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/stevenschaefer/" display-uri="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>2</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>12</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:source-path>/Users/stevenschaefer/forest/trees/public/sss-001Y.tree</fr:source-path>
                <fr:uri>https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-001Y/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>sss-001Y</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/sss-001Y/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="A Grammar of Finite Cardinals">A Grammar of Finite Cardinals</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
  Define a grammar for each finite cardinal <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n : \mathbb {N}]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
                <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ [0] \colonequals  \bot  ]]></fr:tex>
                <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[ [\mathsf {suc}(n)] \colonequals  \top  \oplus  [ n ]]]></fr:tex>
                <html:p>
Then define the grammar of all finite cardinals as
<fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathsf {Fin} \colonequals  \textstyle \mathchoice {\textstyle \oplus _{{\mathopen {}\left (n : \mathbb {N}\right )\mathclose {}}}}{\textstyle \oplus _{{\mathopen {}\left (n : \mathbb {N}\right )\mathclose {}}}}{\scriptstyle \oplus _{{\mathopen {}\left (n : \mathbb {N}\right )\mathclose {}}}}{\scriptscriptstyle \oplus _{{\mathopen {}\left (n : \mathbb {N}\right )\mathclose {}}}}. [n]]]></fr:tex></html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors>
              <fr:author>
                <fr:link href="/stevenschaefer/" title="Steven Schaefer" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/stevenschaefer/" display-uri="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
              </fr:author>
            </fr:authors>
            <fr:date>
              <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
              <fr:month>2</fr:month>
              <fr:day>12</fr:day>
            </fr:date>
            <fr:source-path>/Users/stevenschaefer/forest/trees/public/sss-001W.tree</fr:source-path>
            <fr:uri>https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-001W/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>sss-001W</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/sss-001W/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Finite Unambiguity is Not Equivalent to Unambiguity">Finite Unambiguity is Not Equivalent to Unambiguity</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
  For a while I believed finite unambiguity to be equivalent to the other definitions of unambiguity.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/stevenschaefer/" title="Steven Schaefer" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/stevenschaefer/" display-uri="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>2</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>12</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:source-path>/Users/stevenschaefer/forest/trees/public/sss-001V.tree</fr:source-path>
                <fr:uri>https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-001V/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>sss-001V</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/sss-001V/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Finite Unambiguity">Finite Unambiguity</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
  Define a grammar <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> to be <html:em>finitely unambiguous</html:em> if <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A \And  A \cong  A]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
  There is likely a better name for this.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/stevenschaefer/" title="Steven Schaefer" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/stevenschaefer/" display-uri="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>2</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>12</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:source-path>/Users/stevenschaefer/forest/trees/public/sss-001T.tree</fr:source-path>
                <fr:uri>https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-001T/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>sss-001T</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/sss-001T/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Unambiguity as Subterminality">Unambiguity as Subterminality</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
  A grammar <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is <html:em>unambiguous</html:em> if the unique map into the <fr:link href="/sss-001O/" title="Terminal Object" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-001O/" display-uri="sss-001O" type="local">terminal object</fr:link> is a monomorphism. That is, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is a subobject of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\top ]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/stevenschaefer/" title="Steven Schaefer" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/stevenschaefer/" display-uri="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>2</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>12</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:source-path>/Users/stevenschaefer/forest/trees/public/sss-001U.tree</fr:source-path>
                <fr:uri>https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-001U/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>sss-001U</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/sss-001U/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Unambiguity as Unique Map into Codomain">Unambiguity as Unique Map into Codomain</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
  A grammar <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is <html:em>unambiguous</html:em> if for all grammars <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex> and maps <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[e , e' : A \vdash  B]]></fr:tex> we have <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[e = e']]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
In a category with terminal objects, this is equivalent to unambiguity defined via subterminality.
</html:p>
                <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
                  <fr:frontmatter>
                    <fr:authors>
                      <fr:author>
                        <fr:link href="/stevenschaefer/" title="Steven Schaefer" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/stevenschaefer/" display-uri="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                      </fr:author>
                    </fr:authors>
                    <fr:date>
                      <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
                      <fr:month>2</fr:month>
                      <fr:day>12</fr:day>
                    </fr:date>
                    <fr:source-path>/Users/stevenschaefer/forest/trees/public/sss-001T.tree</fr:source-path>
                    <fr:uri>https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-001T/</fr:uri>
                    <fr:display-uri>sss-001T</fr:display-uri>
                    <fr:route>/sss-001T/</fr:route>
                    <fr:title text="Unambiguity as Subterminality">Unambiguity as Subterminality</fr:title>
                    <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
                  </fr:frontmatter>
                  <fr:mainmatter>
                    <html:p>
  A grammar <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is <html:em>unambiguous</html:em> if the unique map into the <fr:link href="/sss-001O/" title="Terminal Object" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-001O/" display-uri="sss-001O" type="local">terminal object</fr:link> is a monomorphism. That is, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is a subobject of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\top ]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
                  </fr:mainmatter>
                </fr:tree>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
  We may use an analogy from the category of sets, however I had missed the infinite case when translating this idea to grammars.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/stevenschaefer/" title="Steven Schaefer" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/stevenschaefer/" display-uri="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>2</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>12</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:source-path>/Users/stevenschaefer/forest/trees/public/sss-001X.tree</fr:source-path>
                <fr:uri>https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-001X/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>sss-001X</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/sss-001X/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Sets That Are Isomorphic to Their Own Product">Sets That Are Isomorphic to Their Own Product</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
  Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex> be a set such that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X \cong  X \times  X]]></fr:tex>. Then either <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex> is the empty set, the singleton set, or an infinite set.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
We may read this intuitively as stating the fixed points of the squaring operation on cardinals has solutions <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[0]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[1]]></fr:tex>, and all infinite cardinals <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\kappa ]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
  It is true that if a grammar is unambiguous, then it is finitely unambiguous. However, the converse does not hold unless the grammar has finitely many parse trees for each string. This finiteness condition is a semantic one. If this proof of unambiguity were to be internalized, then it could maybe be captured through the lens of some <fr:link href="/sss-001Y/" title="A Grammar of Finite Cardinals" uri="https://stevenschaefer.net/sss-001Y/" display-uri="sss-001Y" type="local">grammar of finite cardinals</fr:link>.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
  Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A \colonequals  \textstyle \mathchoice {\textstyle \oplus _{{\mathopen {}\left (n : \mathbb {N}\right )\mathclose {}}}}{\textstyle \oplus _{{\mathopen {}\left (n : \mathbb {N}\right )\mathclose {}}}}{\scriptstyle \oplus _{{\mathopen {}\left (n : \mathbb {N}\right )\mathclose {}}}}{\scriptscriptstyle \oplus _{{\mathopen {}\left (n : \mathbb {N}\right )\mathclose {}}}}. \top ]]></fr:tex>. <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is finitely unambiguous but not unambiguous. The isomorphism bewteen <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A \And  A]]></fr:tex> amounts to building a bijection between <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {N}]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {N} \times  \mathbb {N}]]></fr:tex>, which is straightforward.
</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
